[Music]  I'm Keith Olbermann and this is the  resistance it ultimately does not matter  whether the president does doesn't has  or hasn't fired the special counsel who  is investigating him obviously this is a  simplified overview the grinding wheels  of justice and truth would be better  served with robert muller on the job  rather than with his being just the  latest body on the pile of men Trump has  fired as they gather damning facts about  him but in a larger sense firing Mullen  actually speed Trump's demise because  whatever breathing room Trump might gain  from cashiering Muller and declaring the  investigation over and himself  vindicated it would be more than  counterbalanced by the degree to which  the headline Trump fires another  investigator would move the story of his  alleged corruption obstruction and  madness into the center of the American  view it is hard for you and I to really  grasp this we have fought this man and  fought what he meant and saw clearly  what he would do for more than one year  for some of us exactly two years but we  are not average Americans and I'm not  talking about our political viewpoints  I'm talking about our political  viewership if you will I have never seen  a study of just how much time the  average American spends thinking about  politics each day but I would suspect it  is a lot shorter than the shortest of  these commentaries for better or for  worse to the average American even to  the average American voter this just  isn't necessarily a priority in their  lives a president fires the US Attorney  in New York investigating his associates  business dealings how many million  Americans didn't know this happened the  president then fires the FBI director  investigating his campaign how many  million Americans don't know that  happened well far fewer a president then  tweets he's being investigated for  firing the FBI director then fires the  Special Counsel doing that investigating  how many million Americans won't know  this has happened far fewer still there  is a tipping point in everything in  American culture from reality TV  popularity to political scandal  it is almost  always a lot of people who did not know  about something suddenly finding out  about that something you can't really  establish a single tipping point in the  Watergate scandal but the Saturday night  massacre is probably the closest thing  on a Friday Richard Nixon said he would  refuse a court order to surrender his  White House tapes and he ordered the  special prosecutor to drop the Watergate  case on Saturday afternoon in a news  conference carried live on television  that prosecutor said he would continue  the investigation  anyway and would continue to challenge  the president in court later that same  day the president ordered the Attorney  General to fire the special prosecutor  for defying him the Attorney General  refused and resigned the Deputy Attorney  General refused and either resigned or  was fired it still isn't 100% clear  which Saturday night the third ranking  figure in the Justice Department the  Solicitor General finally fired that  prosecutor and all hell then broke loose  on the Friday before the Saturday night  massacre there had been 10 different  stories on the front page of the New  York Times from the Yom Kippur War to a  shootout in midtown Manhattan to Game  five of the 1973 World Series nothing  about Watergate by the Sunday after the  Saturday night massacre there was an  eight column three line headline about  Watergate and four separate front-page  stories including one just about  impeachment by the Monday after the  Saturday night massacre White House and  congressional and Senate offices were  flooded with telegrams the Watergate  scandal front of mind for millions of  Americans but in its 16th month still an  irrelevant shape on the horizon to most  of us suddenly became the only topic in  politics and just as quickly politics  became the only topic in America would  trump fires Muller unfold precisely that  way it's impossible to tell the world of  media is utterly changed since 1973 we  have a thousand more distractions now  than we did when Richard Nixon had fired  Archibald Cox we have another president  who will lie about anything but on why  Nixon the one we have now can do so  instantaneously via social media and his  true believers will happily reject the  insights of Rosenstein and Muller to  instead swear by the insights of diamond  and silk but to fire Muller is to tempt  a repetition of the Saturday night  massacre and the spread of public  awareness of the investigation that  might not destroy Trump but it cannot  possibly help him and just as  importantly it is to risk a repetition  of the aftermath of the massacre the  part very few people noticed or talked  about then or now within 12 days after  Nixon fired the special prosecutor the  pressure and the outcry had grown so  loud that Nixon had to hire another  special prosecutor the one who would  ultimately help force him from office  resist peace

